tewide polls, expert ratings, or money figures are available yet", "Open race with a term-limited incumbent removes any incumbency effect", "Democratic strength in the Little Rock-based 2nd District does not extend statewide"], "rationale": "Arkansas's fifteen-point Republican lean is the strongest evidence available, with no polls, ratings, or fundraising data to move the number; an open race without an incumbent gives neither side a structural edge, leaving the state comfortably Republican on paper.", "watch": "Once the 2028 nominees are set, early statewide polls and the national environment will show whether the margin narrows or widens from the partisan lean.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains one of the most Republican states on the presidential map, with a partisan lean of roughly fifteen points toward the GOP. With the incumbent president term-limited, both parties will nominate new standard-bearers, and no incumbency advantage applies to either side. No statewide general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan balance.\n\nRecent headlines show Democratic energy in downballot Arkansas races, including polls showing Chris Jones leading in the 2nd Congressional District, but that district is far more Democratic than the state as a whole. National attention on the 2028 Republican primary and prospective Democratic candidates does not yet change Arkansas's fundamentals. The state is expected to back the Republican ticket by a comfortable margin, though the exact size depends on the national environment once nominees are known.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891